\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Basic building blocks of a map rendering stack. Data is taken from a data source, converted into a simplified format (for example a spatial index), and served to a client. The visual appearance is defined by a style and supported by fonts and sprites for text and icons.}}{8}{figure.caption.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces System architecture. The \emph {codegen pipeline} (top) transforms the upstream \texttt {v8.json} style specification through three stages into generated Rust types (\texttt {SerialSpec}) used for typed style manipulation, with the MIR providing spec metadata to the optimiser. The \emph {data pipeline} (bottom) decodes \acs {MVT} tiles and scans them to produce tile statistics that feed into data-driven optimizations passes. Dashed arrows denote query or feedback relationships; render tests validate round-trip correctness}}{23}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Layer merging example}}{33}{figure.caption.21}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Cumulative ablation waterfall for load time. Each bar shows the median change introduced by adding one expression pass on top of all previous passes, aggregated across all 18 scenarios and 15 benchmark styles}}{40}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Marginal contribution of each expression pass to load time reduction}}{40}{figure.caption.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Layer count across cumulative ablation steps. Dead elimination and layer merging produce the largest reductions}}{43}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Style size (raw, gzip, Brotli) across ablation steps}}{43}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Style-driven tile shaving pipeline. The optimised style from Experiments~1--2 and tile statistics jointly produce a pruning advisory. Each \ac {MVT} tile is pruned according to the advisory, transformed, and re-encoded as \ac {MLT}. Style-level dead layer elimination cascades into smaller advisories and thus smaller tiles}}{46}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Encoder and decoder data-flow in \texttt {mlt-core}. \emph {Decoding} (left) descends from an input \texttt {\&[u8]} slice through \texttt {Raw*} (zero-copy views over the wire format), \texttt {Parsed*} (lazily decoded, still columnar), and finally \texttt {Tile*} (row-oriented, ready to consume). \emph {Encoding} (right) ascends from a row-oriented \texttt {Tile*} through \texttt {Staged*} (columnar, not yet encoded) and \texttt {Encoded*} (wire-ready byte buffers) into an \texttt {impl Write} sink via a final zero-copy step. Within the encoding path, a \emph {Profile$\leftrightarrow $Optimiser$\leftrightarrow $Encoder} feedback loop implements the multi-strategy competition described in this section: the profile summarises each \texttt {Staged*} stream, the optimiser uses those summaries to trim the candidate search space and generate per-stream encoder guesses, and the encoder fully determines the bytes committed to \texttt {Encoded*}. The \texttt {Raw*}/\texttt {Parsed*}/\texttt {Staged*}/\texttt {Encoded*}/\texttt {Tile*} families share a common \texttt {<Name>} stem and can be in either an encoded or decoded state, which keeps encoder, decoder, and in-place re-encoding on a single type hierarchy}}{47}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Multi-strategy sort competition. The layer is encoded under each candidate sort order; the encoding with the smallest \texttt {total\_len()} is retained. Spatial strategies are pruned by the bounding-box heuristic for large, widely-spread layers. Within each trial, per-stream encoding competitions select the best integer encoder for every data stream}}{48}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Total encoded mbtiles size of the Germany OpenMapTiles tileset under different MinHash similarity modes and Jaccard thresholds. Lower is better. The hybrid mode (teal) produces the smallest output at every threshold; all modes increase monotonically with threshold as fewer column pairs qualify for shared-dictionary grouping. The y-axis is zoomed to the data range to make the differences visible; the full range spans approximately \qty {6}{\mega \byte } out of \qty {3.14}{\giga \byte } total}}{50}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Total tile size per zoom level across configurations~1--5. The gap between \enquote {style-only} (config~2) and \enquote {style+shaving} (config~4) quantifies the cascade effect; the gap between config~4 and config~5 quantifies the contribution of MLT encoding optimizationss}}{52}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Per-zoom compression ratio across MVT, MLT-Java, and MLT-Rust}}{53}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces Interaction between style optimizations and tile shaving. If the combined reduction exceeds the sum of individual reductions, the optimizationss are synergistic - the style cascade produces advisory savings that shaving alone cannot achieve}}{55}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.12}{\ignorespaces Rendering metrics (load time, FPS) across configurations~1--5, enabling direct comparison with Experiments~1--2 in the combined analysis}}{56}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.13}{\ignorespaces Per-style percentage reduction in style size (raw, gzip, Brotli) across the Maputnik generalisation corpus. Styles are sorted by raw reduction}}{58}{figure.caption.77}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.14}{\ignorespaces Relationship between initial style complexity and achievable size reduction}}{59}{figure.caption.78}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.15}{\ignorespaces Heatmap of load time across scenarios (rows) and ablation steps (columns). Darker cells indicate greater improvement relative to baseline}}{60}{figure.caption.84}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.16}{\ignorespaces Time breakdown across ablation steps: style parse, first tile, first frame, and remaining load time}}{61}{figure.caption.85}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.17}{\ignorespaces Heap memory usage across ablation steps}}{61}{figure.caption.86}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.18}{\ignorespaces Per-style Pareto frontier in \texttt {(gzip\_bytes, pass\_count)} space. Each point is a measured (style, variant) pair: cumulative-step variants populate the $x$-axis monotonically by construction and isolated-mode variants sit at \texttt {pass\_count}$=1$ with varying \texttt {gzip\_bytes}. Non-dominated points are highlighted; dominated points are rendered in grey. Generated by \texttt {thesis/scripts/pareto\_analysis.py} from the \texttt {jsonl} output of the benchmark harness using the latest complete ablation session per style}}{68}{figure.caption.102}%
